## 11. SUPABASE SECURITY

Security is required from the beginning.

- Enable Row Level Security on all tables exposed through the API.
- Public users may read only published, public destination and listing data.
- Never allow anonymous users to read booking requests, private provider contact details, internal notes, or customer information.
- Anonymous booking creation must use a controlled server-side endpoint or server action with validation and rate limiting. Do not grant broad public SELECT, UPDATE, or DELETE privileges on booking tables.
- Admin mutations require verified server-side authorization, not merely a hidden UI.
- Protect all privileged routes and mutations.
- Store Supabase service-role credentials only in server-only environment variables and never in client bundles.
- Validate all user input on the server.
- Protect against spam, abuse, mass submissions, and enumeration of booking references.
- Use unpredictable booking references and avoid exposing sequential database IDs.
- Limit image uploads by file type and size.
- Sanitize rendered user content appropriately.
- Keep private fields out of public queries and responses.
- Avoid logging phone numbers, credentials, tokens, or unnecessary personal data.
- Document a secure bootstrap process for the first administrator. Do not create a hardcoded default admin account.
- Test RLS and authorization with anonymous, ordinary authenticated, and administrator roles.

Do not claim the application is secure until the relevant policies and tests have been implemented.

